\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
The Internet and Product Signaling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 31, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Experimental economists have always been fascinated with the recognized
 problems that arise due asymmetric information, most notably moral hazard.
 Within the framework of industrial organization, moral hazard occurs when
 both sellers' costs and buyers' values depend on product quality.
 The sellers know the quality of their product, while the quality cannot
 be directly observed by buyers.
 One possible solution to this problem involves signaling.
 In fact, there have been many papers analyzing the possible equilibria
 that may arise when signaling is an option.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, I do not believe their framwork can be readily applied to today's
 modern market institutions that have arisen due to the widespread use of
 the Internet.
 Today, buyers are able to leave a product review after purchasing an item,
 and this has an effect on future sales of the good.
 In fact, Amazon has a relatively unknown program known as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Vine
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in which they send free items to a select group of people in exchange for
 reviews of the selected products.
 These individuals are required to submit a review online, whether it be
 positive or negative, on all the products they receive.
 Some experts claim that the reviews from Vine participants could be positively
 biased since they may be more inclined to leave encouraging feedback after
 receiving the product for free.
 Yet, Amazon claims that reviews from Vine participants are actually more
 negative, on average.
 Why does Amazon send out free products just to obtain a few extra reviews?
 Well, according to the Amazon marketing department, even negatively reviewed
 items still sell better than items with no reviews.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, buyers of goods over the Internet are able to discern some of the informati
on concerning the product's quality through user reviews and namebrand recogniti
on.
 Yet, they are limited in their ability to gather all relevant information
 since they unable to physcially examine the product or have face-to-face
 communication with the seller.
 The seller, on the other hand, can invest in the quality of the product,
 but he will be unable to relay a perfect signal to the buyer about the
 product due to the same reasons mentioned previously.
 Because of these issues, will sellers even attempt to invest in the quality
 of their products, or will they instead primarily rely on the recognition
 of their brand?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to answer this question, I propose the following experimental design
 that borrows some characteristics from Miller and Plot (1985), but still
 differs significantly from their construction.
 Sellers possess products that are exogenously given the identification
 of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which will be known to the buyers.
 Sellers can add units of quality to their goods, which is valued by the
 buyers, but this increase in quality is not directly observed by the buyers
 either.
 There is no initial difference in quality between the two types of goods,
 yet sellers having the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 good will have a slightly lower marginal cost of adding units of quality
 to their product.
 In addition, buyers will possess a slightly higher value for products designate
d as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Yet, buyers will have increasing valuation for higher quality products,
 thus allowing generic brands to readily compete with their name-brand counterpa
rts.
 Finally, all buyers will have the same redemption schedule, which will
 depend on both the kind and quality of the unit purchased, and the difference
 in value between 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 goods will be held constant.
 Due to the conditions just described, the possibility of signaling exists
 in the constructed markets, even though buyers can identify the two different
 kinds of goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Furthermore, there will be also be two exogeneously determined buying groups.
 One buyer group will be designated as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reviewers,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 while the other group will be those 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reading reviews.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Now, a particular market experiment will proceed in a sequence of sets
 of two distinguishable trading periods.
 In the first trading period, sellers will interact with the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reviewers,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 who will have the ability to leave a review about the seller and the product
 they purchased once trading concludes.
 In this period, sellers can add quality to a particular unit at a cost
 which depends upon the type of unit they are selling.
 Note that the marginal cost will remain constant for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 type, while the marginal cost will change for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 good in different market experiments.
 In the second trading period, each seller will be given two more units
 to sell, which will be at the same level of quality they had purchased
 in the previous period.
 The same group of sellers now interact with the new group of buyers, who
 are able to read reviews left by the first group of buyers.
 Finally, in order to facilitate a double auction trading mechansim in both
 periods, both buyers and sellers will be given an unique identification
 number at the start of each set of two trading periods.
 To sum up, the set of two trading periods will consist of the following
 two parts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Period 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sellers are exogenously assigned the type of good they will be selling,
 which will be known to the buyers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Buyers are exogenously designated as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reviewers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or those 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reading reviews.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Both buyers and sellers are given a unique identification number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sellers submit how many units of quality to add to their two products.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Trading proceeds according to a double auction mechansim.
 Sellers identify their identification number, the type of good they are
 selling (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

), the number of goods they wish to sell (1 or 2), and the price at which
 they are willing to accept.
 Buyers identify their identification number, the type of good they wish
 to purchase, the number of units they wish to purchase, and the price they
 are willing to pay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
After trading concludes, buyers are given the identification numbers of
 the seller associated with the goods they purchased.
 Then, buyers have the opportunity to leave reviews based on the seller
 they purchased from and the quality of the product they purchased.
 So, in a review, buyers are permitted to identify sellers by their identificait
on number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Period 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The same two groups of sellers now interact with the other group of buyers,
 those who can read the reviews left by the previous group.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Before trading commences, the new group of buyers are able to read reviews
 left by the previous group.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Trading proceeds in the same manner implemented in the previous period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, the design described above is associated with the main
 treament of the experiment.
 The control will consist of the same basic design, except that buyers will
 not be separated into two separate groups, and they will not be given the
 option of reviewing a product.
 Instead, buyers will now be able to discern both the kind of good they
 are purchasing, as well as the quality of the good.
 So, during the double auction mechanism, the sellers will be required to
 announce the units of quality of each of their goods, and the buyers will
 announce the units of quality they would like to purchase.
 Using the treatment design I have described, I want to see if there will
 be any changes in quality investment by sellers or the price at which goods
 sell at if buyers are given the option to leave reviews.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreove, I can include variations to the main treatment design in order
 to add more structure and real-world complexity to the experiment, as well
 as answer other interesting questions associated with product reviews and
 purchasing goods using the Internet.
 For example:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Control: In the second trading period, sellers are unable to add additional
 units of quality to their two goods; Treatment: Prior to the second trading
 period, sellers are able to read one randomly selected review left by a
 buyer.
 They can then add additional units of quality to their products in the
 second period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Control: All buyers in the first trading period are required to leave some
 type of review of all the products they purchase; Treament: Buyers in the
 first period are no longer required to leave a review.
 Instead, there is now a small cost that buyers have to pay in order to
 leave a review.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Control: All buyers in the first trading period are required to leave some
 type of review of all the products they purchase; Treatment: Buyers in
 the first period are no longer required to leave a review.
 Instead, sellers can now give away any of their unsold units to buyers
 in exchange for a review after trading concludes.
 If a buyer receives a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

free
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 unit from a seller, then they will be required to leave a review.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Control: The identifaction number of sellers change for each new set of
 two trading periods; Treatment: Sellers maintain the same identifcation
 number throughout the sequence of sets of two trading periods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The possible designs listed above are just variations of the main designed
 I have described in the majority of this proposal, and they do not have
 to be incorporated into the experiment if I so choose.
 I have only mentioned them to identify other possible designs that could
 be implemented in order to answer additional questions associated with
 the topic of product reviews and Internet purchases.
\end_layout

